"""Restore the selected views and give each takeaway a result and its meaning."""

ABSTRACT='''What do averages and snapshots hide about inequality, relationships and change over time? We examine this question through separate country-internet and daily-weather investigations. The Infovis experiments compare network layouts, interactive bundled parallel coordinates, two treemap hierarchies, horizon plots and ridgelines. The SciVis experiments compare color mapping, contour intervals and wind-arrow choices. Further views add country trajectories, fixed starting groups, within-region differences, regional gains, NodeTrix groups, complete-month changes, rainfall concentration and shared weather conditions. Among 175 matched countries, broadband and internet use have a rank correlation of 0.831 for their levels but only 0.054 for their changes. About 47% of measured country variation in 2020 lies within regions. Across the sampled 92-day weather area, hot-dry conditions account for 16.27% of weighted grid-cell days, while the longest local run reaches 67 days. Adding the wind condition narrows joint occurrence to 2.57%. Restoring individual values, timing and location changes what can be learned from a summary.'''

TAKEAWAYS={
1:'India keeps the same six linked countries even when the circular layout puts some of them far away. Distance on the page is therefore not a reliable definition of a neighbor. The links describe similarity in the four measured country indicators; the layout only decides where to draw them.',
2:'South Africa and China both make large gains in internet use, but their fixed-broadband gains are very different: about 0.7 versus 24.6 subscriptions per 100. A single endpoint would hide that difference. The annual paths show why similar progress does not imply the same infrastructure history.',
3:'The enlarged G7 matrix reveals 18 of 28 possible links, with both dense rows and visible gaps. Cuba and Palestine each connect to six group members, while Laos connects to three. Group membership therefore does not mean that every pair is linked. NodeTrix makes those individual relationships checkable inside an otherwise crowded network.',
4:'All six selected countries combine at least 70% internet use with at most five fixed-broadband subscriptions per 100, but their other measurements differ sharply. Cuba has about 59.6 mobile subscriptions per 100, compared with 165.0 in Libya. One shared label such as high adoption and low broadband still hides different country profiles.',
5:'Highlighting six countries while fading the other 169 makes the selection visible without losing its wider context. These countries meet the same adoption and broadband filters, yet spread apart on landlines and mobile subscriptions. Brushing identifies an interesting exception group; the axis intersections, rather than the bundled curves between them, provide its measured values.',
6:'Fixed broadband has a stronger rank association with internet use than mobile does, 0.83 versus 0.53, while landlines and broadband also move together strongly across countries, at 0.87. The scatter cells preserve exceptions that a coefficient alone would hide. These are relationships between 2020 country levels, not explanations of how each country changed.',
7:'Internet use rises while landline subscriptions fall in 126 of 175 matched countries, or 72%, and the combination appears in every region. This is a result about changes within the same countries, rather than a comparison of their current levels. The bars show how widespread the direction is, while leaving the size and cause of each change open.',
8:'Asia contains about 56.9% of the included internet users, but a large rectangle does not mean that a large percentage of its population is online. Area answers how many users live there; country color answers how widely access has spread. Both are needed to distinguish population size from adoption.',
9:'The 50-below-75% band contains slightly more included users than the 75-100% band, 39.7% versus 38.1%. Many online users therefore live in countries where a substantial share of people remain offline. Changing the hierarchy exposes that fact while keeping every country value and the overall total unchanged.',
10:'By 2020, 86.5% of included European countries had reached 75% internet use, compared with 2.2% of African countries. The steps show the timing behind this difference, rather than only its final size. First reaching a milestone is useful evidence of progress, but it does not describe every later value.',
11:'About 47% of measured country variation in 2020 lies within regions, so knowing the regional mean leaves nearly half of this spread unseen. The country dots show what that means in practice: members of one region can have very different adoption rates. This adds information that a single regional average cannot provide.',
12:'Asia gains 26.55 points when its two regional medians are subtracted, but 19.75 points when each country\'s gain is calculated first and then the median is taken. The two answers describe different things. To discuss a typical country\'s progress, preserve its paired observations before summarizing the changes.',
13:'Singapore reaches majority internet use in 2003, China in 2015 and Namibia in 2020, while Tanzania has no observed crossing by the endpoint. The horizon rows keep these different journeys visible in a compact space. Red means below 50%, so it must not be mistaken for declining adoption.',
14:'Europe\'s 2020 median is about 86.9%, compared with 31.9% in Africa, and the rows also show different times of crossing 50%. A regional trajectory summarizes both timing and level, but still hides its member countries. It should be read alongside the individual paths and within-region spread.',
15:'The country median rises from 2.2% to 71.4%, yet the middle half of 2020 values still spans about 37.5 percentage points. Broad improvement therefore does not mean countries have reached similar levels. Reading the distribution alongside the median reveals the uneven progress that one average would hide.',
16:'The global shift toward higher internet use contains different regional histories: Europe moves toward high values earlier, while lower and middle values remain more prominent in Africa. The width of each distribution also matters. In small groups such as Oceania, check the individual country ticks before treating a smooth bump as a stable pattern.',
17:'The same rainfall measurements look different on linear and logarithmic scales because each assigns contrast differently. Linear color emphasizes large absolute amounts; logarithmic color separates smaller positive amounts. A visually stronger feature is therefore not automatically more rain. The color bar and the question being asked must guide the reading.',
18:'Daily rescaling makes each date\'s internal differences clearer, but the same bright color can then mean different temperatures on different dates. A fixed scale preserves the between-date comparison. For an animation about changing temperature, that consistency matters more than making every frame use its full color range.',
19:'Five-degree contours reveal more temperature boundaries than ten-degree contours, while filled bands make broad warm and cool areas easier to compare. The underlying grid is identical in all panels. More visible lines therefore mean a finer display choice, rather than new observations or better source resolution.',
20:'Mean daily maximum temperature falls from 31.19 degrees in July to 26.55 in September, while the maps show where warmth remains. Shared colors make that cooling directly comparable across months. The result describes monthly averages; daily ribbons and hot-day frequencies are needed to understand the episodes hidden inside them.',
21:'The monthly comparison shows cooling across 99.66% of the study area, with an area-weighted mean change of -4.64 degrees. Using only the first and last sampled days gives the opposite sign in 18.26% of the area. Two daily snapshots can therefore misrepresent a change that is clear when complete months are used.',
22:'A smoothly changing daily mean can still hide large temperature differences between places, which appear in the ribbons. Keeping every day also reveals warm and cool episodes within a month. The bands describe spatial spread, so they should not be read as uncertainty around the mean line.',
23:'July and August have similar weighted mean rain totals, about 75 mm, but the share concentrated into five days differs. September has a lower total and the largest concentration share: 77.6% of eligible area receives at least half its rain in one five-day window. Amount alone does not describe how rain arrived.',
24:'The weighted mean frequency of days reaching 35 degrees falls from 22.8% in July to 8.9% in September. These maps count repeated heat across all days, rather than show one selected event. They still cannot tell whether those hot days occur together in one long spell; that requires a run-length calculation.',
25:'Keeping all 92 dates reveals short warm and rainy episodes that monthly maps smooth over, and shows their east-west extent. The space-saving step is averaging across latitude, which removes north-south detail. Use the diagram for timing across longitude and return to maps when the exact location matters.',
26:'Denser arrows retain more locations but can hide direction through overlap. Equal-length arrows make direction easier to compare, while speed-scaled arrows also encode magnitude. These are different reading tasks, so neither setting is universally best. In every case the arrows point toward the flow, and color retains speed information.',
27:'The most frequent wind-source sector changes from southwest in July to south in August and September. Separate monthly roses reveal that shift, while the stacked speed bands show which strengths contribute. Their common radial scale supports comparison, but the roses summarize frequencies and do not locate the winds on a map.',
28:'The combined maps preserve where temperature, rain and wind occur together, which separate area averages lose. The selected dates contrast hot-dry coverage of 34.56% and 1.75%. They illustrate a spatial difference; the complete-period frequency calculation is still needed to judge how common the shared conditions are.',
29:'Dry conditions occur on about 76.65% of weighted grid-cell days, yet the hot-dry-windy combination occurs on only 2.57%. A common individual condition does not imply common joint occurrence. The overlap must be checked at the same cell and date, and the first three overlapping bars must not be added.',
30:'Hot-dry conditions account for 16.27% of weighted grid-cell days, while one location has a 67-day uninterrupted run. Frequency, local duration and affected area therefore answer different questions. Reporting them together, with the threshold comparison, shows more than a single event count and makes the chosen definition visible.'
}

def refine(figures,why,more,captions):
    by={f['figure']:f for f in figures}
    f=by[5]
    f['path']='output/internet_revision/pcp_focus.png'
    f['title']='Bundled country profiles with a highlighted selection.'
    f['explanation'][0]='Where do the six selected countries sit among all 175 profiles?'
    f['explanation'][1]='Each background line is one of 175 countries in 2020. Region gives the line color. Darker bundled lines highlight the six countries meeting both shaded brushes: at least 70% internet use and at most five fixed-broadband subscriptions per 100. Their names appear below. Each axis retains its own full-range scale.'
    why[5]='Figure 4 makes six countries easy to read individually. This view puts the other countries back as faint context, so the reader can see where that selection sits in the full dataset. Brushing performs the two filters together; bundling gathers curves toward regional centers while preserving their measured axis intersections. The two figures serve a detail-to-context comparison, rather than isolating bundling alone, because the axis ranges and color assignment also differ.'
    f['explanation'][2]=why[5]
    f['detailed_interpretation']='Only six of the 175 countries pass both filters: Bhutan, Cuba, Kuwait, Kyrgyzstan, Libya and South Africa. Their high adoption intersections sit beside the many lighter country profiles, while all six remain close to the bottom of the broadband axis. They spread farther apart on mobile and landline subscriptions. The highlighted group is therefore similar on the two selected criteria, rather than identical on all four measures.\n\nThe faint lines are still observations, not a shaded uncertainty band. They provide the population of country profiles against which the selection is judged. Line color identifies a supplied region, so several selected countries share a color. The names below identify the full selection; precise country values can be checked in Figure 4 or by hovering in the browser.\n\nThe wider axes here preserve the full-country context. Figure 4 uses narrower ranges to make the six profiles readable, so the apparent line slopes must not be compared between the figures. The curves also represent no values between axes: only the intersections are measurements.'
    captions[5]='Six brushed profiles emphasized against all 175 countries, 2020; color shows region and curves use 50% regional bundling.'
    f=by[6]
    f['path']='images/quality/telecom_scatterplot_matrix.png'
    f['title']='Four telecom measures: associations and exceptions.'
    f['explanation'][0]='What does the complete set of pairwise relationships reveal?'
    f['explanation'][1]='The four variables occupy the same row and column order: internet use, landlines, fixed broadband and mobile. Diagonal cells are country-count histograms. Below the diagonal, each dot is one country, with the column variable on the horizontal axis and the row variable on the vertical axis. Color shows region. Above the diagonal, the matching pair has its Spearman rank-correlation value. All cells use the same 175 countries in 2020.'
    why[6]='The two adoption scatterplots in A1 already compared broadband and mobile. Restoring the full scatterplot matrix adds the four other pairs, the individual distributions and a common sample. Each relationship can be checked in its own scatter cell rather than inferred from a single correlation number. This also gives a position-based check on the four-variable parallel-coordinate profiles.'
    f['explanation'][2]=why[6]
    f['detailed_interpretation']='Start with the broadband row and internet-use column: the upward cloud corresponds to the 0.83 value above the diagonal. The mobile-versus-adoption pair is more scattered and has a lower value, 0.53. This repeats A1\'s broad observation, but now uses 2020 matched-country data and rank correlation, rather than the 2024 adoption comparison.\n\nThe rest of the matrix adds the information that the two-panel version omitted. Landlines and broadband have the strongest pairwise rank association, about 0.87. Landlines and adoption are also strongly related, at 0.79, while mobile has weaker relationships with landlines and broadband, about 0.48 and 0.49. The diagonal histograms show many countries near low fixed-infrastructure values, while mobile counts spread well above 100 subscriptions per 100 people. Such counts can exceed 100 because they measure subscriptions rather than unique people.\n\nSpearman correlation compares the ordering of countries: values closer to 1 indicate a more consistently increasing relationship. It does not require the points to lie on a straight line. Check the scatter cell as well as the coefficient for exceptions and shape. The colors show regional composition, but these pooled coefficients do not describe a separate within-region relationship.\n\nFor 2015-2020 gains, broadband and internet-use changes have a rank correlation of only about 0.054. Thus, even the stronger 2020 level relationship cannot explain each country\'s progress. Figure 7 moves from those levels to a direct count of matched-country changes.'
    captions[6]='All six variable pairs for 175 countries in 2020, with country-count histograms on the diagonal and Spearman rank correlations above it.'
    by[19]['path']='output/internet_revision/contours_worked.png'
    by[19]['detailed_interpretation']+='\n\nThe inset uses one actual August 22 grid cell. Its corner temperatures straddle 35 degrees. Linear interpolation locates the two edge crossings; joining them gives the contour segment for that cell. Repeating this step across neighboring cells traces the boundary.'
    by[3]['detailed_interpretation']+='\n\nTo read one relationship, choose a country on the left of the enlarged matrix and trace across to another country\'s column. A teal cell means that the original graph contains that pair\'s link; a white cell means it does not. The same pair appears again on the other side of the diagonal because the links have no direction. Count only one triangle when counting the group\'s 18 links. Gray diagonal cells compare a country with itself and are excluded.\n\nThe seven groups come from the similarity graph: the grouping algorithm favors sets with more connection weight inside than between them. A group is therefore not a continent or an income class. Its members were grouped using the four scaled indicators. The small matrices preserve internal links, while the gray lines between matrices preserve cross-group links. This hybrid view lets the reader keep both the overall graph and its local structure.'
    by[9]['detailed_interpretation']+='\n\nFor example, moving a country from a region-first branch to an adoption-first branch does not change its user count or adoption color. It changes which countries surround it and which total the parent heading summarizes. Compare the band totals at the root before zooming into a branch; zooming changes the displayed denominator.'
    by[12]['detailed_interpretation']+='\n\nRead one region across both panels. First subtract its two endpoint medians in the left panel. Then find the open median-gain circle in the right panel and compare it with the black endpoint-shift tick. Their separation is the visible answer to the question. Each small dot on the right is one country\'s paired change; the smooth shape summarizes those dots rather than adding observations.'
    by[13]['detailed_interpretation']+='\n\nA horizon plot folds bands back into the same row. Here a first band covers 0-25 percentage points from 50%, and a second, darker band covers the next 25 points. Read the sign from red or blue, then the band darkness and visible height. Do not add the two sides as if they were separate countries. This folding saves height but makes the printed endpoint labels useful for exact values.'
    by[15]['detailed_interpretation']+='\n\nRead each ridge from left to right as low to high adoption. A bulge marks a range where many country values lie close together. The smooth curve spreads each observation over a small neighborhood, so it is an estimated shape, not a count at every percentage. The country ticks below it show the actual values; the common horizontal scale carries the comparison across years.'
    for f in figures:
        f['explanation'][4]=TAKEAWAYS[f['figure']]
        f['caption']=captions[f['figure']]
